 "lines": [
  {
   "start": 0.18,
   "end": 2.9,
   "text": "Here's why starting early matters more than starting big."
  },
  {
   "start": 2.9,
   "end": 7.86,
   "text": "Say you put $10,000 somewhere, it grows 7% a year, and you never touch it."
  },
  {
   "start": 7.86,
   "end": 11.42,
   "text": "After 10 years, you have about $19,700."
  },
  {
   "start": 11.42,
   "end": 17.84,
   "text": "After 20 years, about $38,700, after 30 years, about $76,000."
  },
  {
   "start": 17.84,
   "end": 22.06,
   "text": "Now compare that to simple interest, where you only earn on the original $10,000."
  },
  {
   "start": 22.06,
   "end": 24.31,
   "text": "After 30 years, that's $31,000."
  },
  {
   "start": 24.31,
   "end": 26.22,
   "text": "Same rate, same money."
  },
  {
   "start": 26.22,
   "end": 28.42,
   "text": "Compounding more than doubles it."
  },
  {
   "start": 28.6,
   "end": 31.48,
   "text": "There's a shortcut for this called the Rule of 72."
  },
  {
   "start": 31.48,
   "end": 36.12,
   "text": "Divide 72 by your growth rate, and that's roughly how many years it takes your money to double."
  },
  {
   "start": 36.12,
   "end": 38.76,
   "text": "At 7%, that's about 10 years."
  },
  {
   "start": 38.76,
   "end": 43.22,
   "text": "The math doesn't care how much you start with, it cares how long you leave it alone."
  }
 ]
}